In LaTeX, you can create a new line within a cell of a table by using the `\\` command. This command is used to start a new line within a cell. Here is a simple example of how to create a table with a new line within a cell:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{array}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|} 
\hline
Header 1 & Header 2 \\ \hline
First cell & This is the first line of the cell. \\ 
          & This is the second line of the cell. \\ \hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

In this example, the second cell of the first row has two lines of text. The `\\` command is used to insert a new line within the cell. The `l` in the tabular specification stands for left-aligned cells, and the `|` characters create vertical lines between the columns. The `\\` command is used to insert a new line within the cell. If you need more control over the formatting, you can use the `p{}` column type to specify a fixed width for the cell:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{array}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|p{3cm}|p{3cm}|} 
\hline
Header 1 & Header 2 \\ \hline
First cell & This is the first line of the cell. \\ 
          & This is the second line of the cell. \\ \hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `p{3cm}` column type is used to create cells with a fixed width of 3cm. The `\\` command is used to insert a new line within the cell.

In C++, if you want to run the same code inline on multiple named variables, you can use the `for_each` algorithm from the `<algorithm>` header along with an `std::vector` or any other container that holds your variables. This allows you to apply the same operation to each element in the container efficiently. Here's a concise example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <functional>

int main() {
    // Initialize a vector of variables
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    // Define a function to apply to each element
    auto square = [](int& num) { num = num * num; };

    // Apply the function to each element in the vector
    std::for_each(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), square);

    // Output the results
    for (const auto& num : numbers) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example:
- We have a `std::vector<int>` containing some initial values.
- We define an inline lambda function `square` that squares each element.
- We use `std::for_each` to apply this lambda function to each element in the vector.
- Finally, we print the squared values.

This method is concise and leverages C++'s standard library to handle the iteration and transformation inline across multiple variables. If your variables are not in a container, you might need to use other techniques like recursion or manually looping, but using containers and algorithms like `std::for_each` is generally the most efficient and idiomatic approach.

