Certainly! To achieve this, you can use HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to create an input field with a search icon that is clickable. Below is an example of how you can do this:

### HTML
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Search Icon</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="search-container">
        <input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search...">
        <button id="searchIcon" class="icon-btn">🔍</button>
    </div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### CSS (styles.css)
```css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

.search-container {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
}

#searchInput {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.icon-btn {
    margin-left: 10px;
    padding: 5px 10px;
    border: none;
    background-color: #f0f0f0;
    cursor: pointer;
    border-radius: 4px;
    transition: background-color 0.3s;
}

.icon-btn:hover {
    background-color: #e0e0e0;
}
```

### JavaScript (script.js)
```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', (event) => {
    const searchInput = document.getElementById('searchInput');
    const searchIcon = document.getElementById('searchIcon');

    searchIcon.addEventListener('click', () => {
        // Call the function when the icon is clicked
        performSearch();
    });

    searchInput.addEventListener('input', () => {
        // You can add more logic here if needed
    });

    function performSearch() {
        const query = searchInput.value;
        if (query) {
            console.log('Search query:', query);
            // Add your search logic here
        }
    }
});
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML**: 
   - The `div` with class `search-container` wraps the input and the icon button.
   - The input field has an ID `searchInput` and a placeholder text "Search...".
   - The button has an ID `searchIcon` and contains the reading glasses icon (🔍).

2. **CSS**:
   - The `.search-container` uses Flexbox to align the input and the icon button.
   - The `#searchInput` is styled to take up most of the space.
   - The `.icon-btn` is styled to look like a button with a hover effect.

3. **JavaScript**:
   - The script waits for the DOM to load.
   - It adds an event listener to the icon button (`searchIcon`) to call the `performSearch` function when clicked.
   - It also adds an event listener to the input field (`searchInput`) so you can add more logic if needed.
   - The `performSearch` function logs the current value of the input field to the console. You can replace this with your actual search logic.

You can customize the `performSearch` function to handle your specific search functionality. This setup ensures that the icon is clickable and triggers the desired function when clicked.

